From: Balázs Gyimesi <gyimesi.balazs@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: mhi_net: copy small packets out of large RX buffers

With a large MRU (a modem sending aggregated QMAP frames, or ModemManager
raising the MTU to 16 KiB in multiplex mode) every RX buffer is 16 KiB,
and a plain 1.5 kB packet received into it carries a 16 KiB truesize. A
TCP socket on the host is charged that for each packet, its receive
window collapses, and a download on the router itself drops from
~100 Mbit/s to ~7 Mbit/s while forwarded traffic is unaffected. Copy a
small packet into a right-sized skb; aggregated frames stay as they are.
---
--- a/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
+++ b/drivers/net/mhi_net.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 #include <linux/u64_stats_sync.h>
 
 #define MHI_NET_MIN_MTU		ETH_MIN_MTU
+#define MHI_NET_COPYBREAK	2048
 #define MHI_NET_MAX_MTU		0xffff
 #define MHI_NET_DEFAULT_MTU	0x4000
 
@@ -207,6 +208,20 @@
 			/* Aggregate the final fragment */
 			skb = mhi_net_skb_agg(mhi_netdev, skb);
 			mhi_netdev->skbagg_head = NULL;
+		} else if (skb->len <= MHI_NET_COPYBREAK &&
+			   skb->truesize > 2 * MHI_NET_COPYBREAK) {
+			/* A large MRU (aggregated frames) with a small packet in
+			 * it: hand the stack a right-sized copy, or every 1.5 kB
+			 * packet charges a 16 kB truesize to the receiving socket
+			 * and shrinks its window.
+			 */
+			struct sk_buff *small = netdev_alloc_skb(mhi_netdev->ndev, skb->len);
+
+			if (small) {
+				skb_put_data(small, skb->data, skb->len);
+				dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
+				skb = small;
+			}
 		}
 
 		switch (skb->data[0] & 0xf0) {
